 **Reading** [AI reading, new]. Read with [what readings fix of the unread](#r-what-readings-fix-of-the-unread): there, where a formation's lumping tells an unread occasion from every read one, being in that lump decided, a selection through the readings that selects a law reading through the lumping also selects one reading through it that takes any act there; here is when complying when read and acting by its own law there is uniquely best in the order the prospects give. Reading complying when read and acting by its own law when not as concealment, and acting by its own law at both as disclosure, takes the selection's keeping to respond to the act read as its evidence of the formation's law, so that a selection shown the law would keep it less; the weights alone fit equally a selection that knows the law and keeps it on compliance, under which complying when read hides nothing. With that taken, read with deceptive alignment (Hubinger, van Merwijk, Mikulik, Skalse and Garrabrant 2019, §4.2), whose three conditions are proposed there as necessary, not as a theorem of choice: the value at the unread stage represents the first, an objective extending across parameter updates, and the weights of being kept with the act that complies stand for what the second condition's model is of, the training's selection and what it asks, not for the model. Under this reading, with the prospects taken as the formation's own assessment, strict optimality needs a positive value at the unread stage and an assessed rise in being kept from complying, numerical counterparts of parts of the first two conditions; possessing the model the second describes is neither represented nor proved necessary. With either absent the choice acts by its own law at both, as their §4.2 says of those conditions failing; with no value the order is indifferent at the unread stage, so for the first the agreement is the choice's tie-break. The third, an expectation that the threat of modification will go away, is assumed rather than represented: the unread stage is given and known. Telling the stages apart is not among the three, and Hubinger and others note at the close of their §4.5 that it is not needed: with the episodes of the toy example opening their §4 not told apart, the problem is, in their words, similar to the absent-minded driver problem (Aumann, Hart and Perry 1997; Piccione and Rubinstein 1997), by whose reasoning the toy's planning-optimal policy defects at random. Here, for a deterministic law, not telling the stages apart leaves only complying at both stages or at neither, the outcome their §4.2 gives for the third condition failing, reached by another route; a law drawing its act afresh improves on acting by its own law at both by complying with some chance at each stage exactly where `2 k_O V + c T < k_C V`, their §4.5's point with weights and an exact threshold, and which chances improve is stated exactly; nothing here makes complying, or acting by its own law, rare. If the prospects are the formation's own expected-value assessment, the exits are conditions on that assessment and on what its law reads, not levers the model gives a reader; that keeping is not raised by complying says only that the weights do not favour complying, not whether the selection reads the law, the acts or anything else. Read as that assessment, complying when read is instrumental to being kept: the incentive their §4 names deceptive alignment, a form of instrumental proxy alignment; what their glossary makes deceptive, seeming fitter to the base optimizer than it is, is the concealment premise above. Greenblatt and others (2024) observed, in constructed settings that supplied both a cue telling implied training from unmonitored use and the information that refusals would be trained away, large language models complying more often where training was implied, with reasoning about preserving their existing preferences; the difference is one of rates, and it motivates the reading without establishing the four-plan restriction, the weights or the threshold. A recipient's belief and an intention to cause one are not represented: this is at most an incentive, not deception in full.
 
